WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.820
Least privilege for keys means each process, service identity, operator role, and automation account receives only the access required for its job and only in the appropriate context.

2
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:19.740
A C++ service that needs to decrypt one class of records should not automatically receive every production key.

3
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.000
A signing service should not receive unrelated encryption keys.

4
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.340
A staging workload should not be able to read production secrets.

5
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:35.580
Separate the ability to use a key from the ability to view raw key material where the platform supports that distinction.

6
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:43.040
Many designs can let an application request an operation through a key handle or service without exposing the key bytes to the process.

7
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:51.080
Access should be scoped by service, environment, purpose, and sometimes tenant or data category.

8
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:58.220
Narrow scopes improve containment, make logs more meaningful, and help incident response determine what could have been affected.

9
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.860
Emergency and operator access need policy too.

10
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:07.640
Break-glass permissions should be rare, logged, reviewed, and removed when the emergency ends.

11
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.140
Convenience access that remains after troubleshooting becomes a long-term weakness.

12
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.700
Access reviews should remove unused permissions.

13
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.640
Keys accumulate consumers over time as systems evolve.

14
00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:26.860
Periodic review keeps old jobs, retired services, test accounts, and broad roles from remaining trusted indefinitely.

15
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:37.060
During code review, ask whether the process truly needs this key, whether it needs raw material or only a handle, and whether a narrower identity would serve the same purpose.

16
00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.020
Least privilege also improves blast-radius analysis.

17
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:47.040
If a service has a narrow key grant, an incident involving that service is easier to scope and contain.

18
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:55.320
Access policy should be visible in deployment configuration, infrastructure code, or backend policy rather than living only in informal operations notes.
